-- Passage rg10-text-structure-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e267a807-f781-5eee-ba03-5b355b6ecc5e', 'rg10-text-structure-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Moss on Ferro Street$q$, $q$When a researcher in Varden City announced that a single wall of moss could trap as much fine dust as a row of street trees, the news spread quickly through the local press. Within weeks, three shop owners on Ferro Street had hung moss panels beside their doors, and the city council asked whether every bus shelter should get one. The idea was appealing because moss is cheap, quiet and green. Yet the full story, as the researcher herself later explained, is more complicated than the headlines suggested.

Dr. Anika Rautio of the Varden Institute of Plant Science had measured dust levels on both sides of a moss panel for one week in early spring. The air behind the panel held about a third less fine dust than the air in front of it. Moss leaves are tiny and densely packed, and their surfaces carry a slight electrical charge, so particles that drift into them tend to stick.

When her team repeated the measurements through a dry summer, however, the results shifted. The panels captured far less dust in July than in April, and on the hottest days they captured almost none. Moss, it turned out, traps particles well only when it is damp. A dried panel becomes brittle, and some of the dust it had collected blows loose again.

Rautio did not abandon her original finding. Instead, she narrowed it. Moss panels, she now argues, can make a measurable difference in shaded, humid places such as narrow streets, underpasses and the north sides of buildings. In open, sunny spots they need a watering system, which adds cost and makes the idea less attractive.

The Ferro Street panels are still hanging. Two of them, on the shaded side of the street, remain thick and green. The third, which faces south, has turned the color of straw. Together, they make Rautio's revised claim easier to see than any chart could.$q$, 'draft')
