-- Passage rg10-text-structure-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ebacce62-7c11-5437-98a4-6e15f220dbd2', 'rg10-text-structure-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Counting Strokes$q$, $q$For years, coaches at the Riverside Rowing Club in Pellham taught a simple rule: the faster a crew wants to go, the more strokes it should take each minute. Rowers who pulled 36 strokes a minute were expected to beat those who pulled 32. When the club fitted its boats with speed sensors two seasons ago, coach Dariusz Okafor expected the data to confirm the rule.

At first it did. In short sprints of about 250 meters, crews with higher stroke rates were almost always faster. The relationship was so consistent that Okafor printed the chart and pinned it to the boathouse wall.

Over the full 2,000-meter race distance, however, the pattern broke down. Crews that started at very high rates often slowed sharply in the second half, while crews that held a steadier rate of 33 or 34 finished stronger. The sensors showed why: at high rates, rowers shortened each stroke, so every pull moved the boat a little less far. Beyond a certain point, adding strokes added fatigue faster than it added speed.

Okafor now teaches a revised rule. Stroke rate matters, but distance per stroke matters just as much, and the best crews find the highest rate they can sustain without losing length. Training sessions now include drills in which rowers hold a fixed rate and try to cover more water with each stroke.

The chart is still on the boathouse wall, but a second chart hangs beside it, showing the full race. Together, they remind the crews that a rule can be true for part of a race and misleading for the whole of it.$q$, 'draft')
